# Every arithmetic candidate, and whether it lands in exactly one verdict. One row per site.
#
# Checked per candidate, not by totals
#   Checking only that the verdicts add up to the number of candidates passes when a candidate in
#   two verdicts is cancelled by a candidate in none. Each candidate must have exactly one verdict,
#   which is a statement about each candidate, not about the totals. So the law groups by (site,
#   filing, layer) and requires exactly one.
#
# A candidate with no verdict is the silent failure
#   A candidate falling through every CASE branch takes a NULL verdict, and the site then reports
#   a smaller population and a cleaner verdict than it earned: the same failure
#   `violates IS NULL` is guarded against in checks/.
#
# The NULL filter sits inside the count
#   Never beside it. As a `WHERE` it removes the whole group, and a candidate with no verdict stops
#   being a candidate; as a `FILTER` it leaves the group with no verdicts, and `classes <> 1`
#   catches it. The two are one keyword apart, and they make opposite claims.
#
# Which verdicts exist is the type's job
#   No count can do it. "Exactly one verdict" survives any renaming of the verdicts, a misspelt one
#   included, and a tally that selects a verdict by name then drops it. The verdicts are
#   `public.arithmetic_verdict`, and every branch casts to it, so a misspelling fails when the
#   query is parsed, on every run, whether or not any candidate reaches that branch.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `arithmetic_class`. Drop the `ELSE` that makes a remainder
#   `computable` in arithmetic/remainder.sqlc: its candidates fall out of every verdict, and the
#   law must fail for the `remainder` site alone. With the filter beside the count it would still
#   return `t`.
-- arithmetic/all.sqlc, one candidate to exactly one verdict.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT z.site AS subject,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE z.classes <> 1) = 0 AS holds,
           format('%s candidates, %s in exactly one class', count(*),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE z.classes = 1)) AS detail
    FROM ( SELECT site, filing, layer,
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE w.verdict IS NOT NULL) AS classes
           FROM ( :compose(arithmetic/all.sqlc) ) w
           GROUP BY site, filing, layer ) z
    GROUP BY z.site
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'arithmetic_class'
